---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { AUTHOR, AUTHOR_EMAIL } from "../seo";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this site collects, what it does not collect, and what third-party advertising does."
  crumbs={[{ label: "Privacy" }]}
>
  <article>
    <h1>Privacy</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      This is a static site. It has no accounts, no login, no comment system,
      and no server that could store anything about you. The pages are files on
      a CDN.
    </p>

    <h2>What the site itself stores</h2>
    <p>
      The guide sets no cookies of its own, and runs no server that could log
      your visits. Your browser keeps four choices in its local storage: light
      or dark, the palette, an open or closed sidebar, and decorations on or
      off. A fifth value shows that the decoration animation played in this
      tab. The browser deletes that value when you close the tab. These values stay in your browser, and the site never
      sends them anywhere. Code that you run in a playground runs entirely
      in your browser as WebAssembly. The code is never uploaded. When you
      leave the page, your browser discards the edits that you made to a
      snippet. The search index is a static JSON file. Your browser downloads
      the file and searches it locally, so the text that you type into the
      search box is never sent to a server.
    </p>

    <h2>Measurement</h2>
    <p>
      The site uses Google Analytics to count how many people read it, which
      chapters they read, and approximately where in the world they are.
      Google Analytics records these items:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the page that you are on</li>
      <li>the link that sent you here</li>
      <li>your browser and your device type</li>
      <li>a location at the level of a country, derived from your IP address</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Google Analytics sets a cookie, so that it counts two page loads from the
      same browser as one visit and not two. There is no login here, and
      nothing asks for your name or email. Thus none of this data is tied to an
      identity.
    </p>
    <p>
      To stop the measurement entirely, block the domains{" "}
      <code>googletagmanager.com</code> and <code>google-analytics.com</code>,
      or use a browser that blocks trackers. Nothing on the site needs the
      measurement. Every page, playground and search works in the same way
      with it or without it.
    </p>

    {
      /*
       * Conditional on purpose, and it has to stay that way until AdSense
       * approves the site. The ad script is emitted on every page (see
       * `ADSENSE_CLIENT` in src/adsense.ts), so Google may already set a
       * cookie here, but no ad is being served and "the site is funded by
       * Google AdSense" was a present-tense claim that was not true.
       *
       * This cannot be derived from `ADSENSE_CLIENT`. That constant says
       * whether the tag is emitted, not whether the account is approved, and
       * today those two facts disagree. After approval, change "may use" to
       * "uses" and "may use cookies" to "use cookies" by hand.
       */
    }
    <h2>Advertising</h2>
    <p>
      This site may use Google AdSense to show advertisements. Where
      advertising is enabled, Google and its advertising partners may use
      cookies and device identifiers to serve and measure ads. Where Google has
      a legal basis, they may also use cookies and device identifiers to
      personalise ads, based on your
      earlier visits to this site and to other sites.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can disable personalised advertising at{" "}
      <a href="https://adssettings.google.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Google Ad Settings</a>. Google explains how it uses data from sites that
      use its services, on its{" "}
      <a
        href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >partner sites policy</a> page. To refuse third-party vendor cookies from
      more companies, go to{" "}
      <a href="https://optout.aboutads.info" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >aboutads.info</a>. You can also block cookies in your browser. The
      guide itself does not need cookies.
    </p>

    <h2>Third parties the pages contact</h2>
    <p>
      Ad requests go to the advertising domains of Google. Analytics beacons go
      to the measurement domains of Google. This domain serves everything else
      that the site needs, for example the compiler artifacts and the editor.
      Links to other sites, for example ziglang.org or Codeberg, are ordinary
      links. If you click one, you visit that site under its own policy.
    </p>

    <h2>YouTube</h2>
    <p>
      The short video cards at the end of a chapter or blog post, and on the
      home page, show riddles and jokes from YouTube. When the site is built,
      the build reads the list of videos from the public feed of the channel.
      The site does not read the feed when you visit.
    </p>
    <p>
      The card at the end of a chapter or post starts to play by itself, with
      the sound off, when half of the card is on your screen. At that time, the
      page loads the YouTube player from <code>youtube-nocookie.com</code>,
      which is the privacy-enhanced mode of YouTube. YouTube then receives your
      IP address and browser details, as any video site does. YouTube may
      store data in your browser when the video plays.{" "}
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >The privacy policy of Google</a> applies to the player. If you never
      scroll to the card, nothing from YouTube loads.
    </p>
    <p>
      The video does not start by itself in these conditions: your system is
      set to reduce motion, or your browser has data saver on. The cards on
      the home page never start by themselves. In those cases, nothing from
      YouTube loads until you press Play. With JavaScript off, Play is a link
      that opens the video on YouTube.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      {AUTHOR} maintains this guide. Send questions about this page to{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${AUTHOR_EMAIL}`}>{AUTHOR_EMAIL}</a>, or to the{" "}
      <a
        href="https://github.com/rajeshpillai/zig-guide"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >issue tracker</a> if the answer is useful to everyone. The{" "}
      <a href={`${base}contact/`}>contact page</a> lists every route.
    </p>

    <p><a href={base}>Back to the guide</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>
